  "notes": "Brooklyn's gospel-organ virtuoso. `confidence` is **low** because the research rates the entry medium-low and says the parametric detail is gospel-idiom inference. The stated parameter is the instrument: \"**Hammond-organ gospel harmony** — plagal and deceptive cadences, tritone substitutions, chromatic passing chords between every diatonic target, and reharmonisation of a repeated melody; drawbar registration and Leslie speed changes function as dynamics.\" Chromatic passing chords *between every diatonic target* is why `chordDurationBeats` runs 1–4 — the shortest of the producer models here — with both `susSubstitutionProb` and `susOrDimProb` raised, and no fixed progression families, because reharmonising a repeated melody is the opposite of a family. Drums are \"live funk/gospel kit — kick on 1 and the offbeat of 2, snare hard on 2&4 with dense ghost 16ths, and a shout-chorus double-time section as the climax device\", so `secondaryAnchor` is 2&, the ghost probability fills all eight 16th positions and `fillBeforeSection` marks the shout chorus. The low end is a genuinely unusual claim and it is why no `bass808` block exists and the bass glide is near zero: \"**the organ's bass pedals or the left hand carry the bass** on much of his work, which gives a legato, portamento-free low end distinct from both played bass and 808\". Melody is \"instrumental and improvised, with the vocal secondary. He sings and plays simultaneously on the solo material\" — density 5–11 with `endVariation` and a raised octave jump. ⚠ Every drum number and every melody figure here is INFERRED. ⚠ The gospel shout chorus (double-time, straight-8th, no backbeat displacement) is a section-level feel change a single model cannot state; the arrangement carries it only as a long solo-and-outro block.",
